WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:03.860
In part one

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.060
the café's four weeks gave the line one

3
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.780
point nine plus one point four t.

4
00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.851
The recipe was the normal equations.

5
00:00:12.851 --> 00:00:15.648
Nothing in it needs a straight line

6
00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:19.050
only a model that is linear in its unknowns.

7
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.270
So add a term, t squared.

8
00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:26.288
Will the best parabola fit better?

9
00:00:26.288 --> 00:00:28.240
Pause and guess.

10
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:36.445
A parabola, C plus D t plus E t squared

11
00:00:36.445 --> 00:00:38.874
adds a third column

12
00:00:38.874 --> 00:00:43.846
the squares of the weeks, zero, one, four

13
00:00:43.846 --> 00:00:44.540
nine.

14
00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.281
And here is the surprise.

15
00:00:47.281 --> 00:00:49.194
The best E is zero

16
00:00:49.194 --> 00:00:52.829
the best parabola is the line itself

17
00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:55.890
with the same error, point two.

18
00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:56.577
Why?

19
00:00:56.577 --> 00:00:59.109
Look at the line's residuals.

20
00:00:59.193 --> 00:01:01.387
Their dot product with the

21
00:01:01.471 --> 00:01:03.074
new column is zero

22
00:01:03.074 --> 00:01:06.280
they are already perpendicular to it.

23
00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.563
So that column has nothing to add.

24
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.979
Usually a new column lowers

25
00:01:12.065 --> 00:01:13.705
the error a little.

26
00:01:13.705 --> 00:01:15.430
It never raises it.

27
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.394
Now go further.

28
00:01:17.394 --> 00:01:19.885
A cubic has four unknowns

29
00:01:19.885 --> 00:01:22.283
and there are four weeks.

30
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:26.250
So the cubic can pass through every point.

31
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.513
The error is zero: a perfect fit.

32
00:01:29.513 --> 00:01:32.956
But continue both curves to week four.

33
00:01:32.956 --> 00:01:36.840
The line says seven hundred and fifty cups.

34
00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:40.574
The cubic turns down and says four hundred.

35
00:01:40.653 --> 00:01:42.620
It has bent itself to the

36
00:01:42.699 --> 00:01:44.430
noise in four numbers.

37
00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.640
So it is tempting to think

38
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.881
more terms fit better

39
00:01:49.881 --> 00:01:53.290
so more terms make a better model.

40
00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:54.370
Wrong.

41
00:01:54.415 --> 00:01:56.907
Adding a column can only enlarge

42
00:01:56.985 --> 00:01:58.308
the column space

43
00:01:58.308 --> 00:02:00.722
so the error can only go down

44
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.448
all the way to zero once there are

45
00:02:03.525 --> 00:02:05.550
as many columns as points.

46
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:07.540
That is overfitting

47
00:02:07.622 --> 00:02:10.575
the cubic's forecast of four hundred

48
00:02:10.657 --> 00:02:12.379
is the noise talking.

49
00:02:12.379 --> 00:02:15.578
Judge a model on data it has not seen

50
00:02:15.578 --> 00:02:18.940
in machine learning, the validation set.

51
00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.184
Squares also magnify big errors.

52
00:02:22.184 --> 00:02:25.681
Suppose week three had a street festival

53
00:02:25.764 --> 00:02:28.262
and the café sold one thousand

54
00:02:28.345 --> 00:02:29.760
two hundred cups.

55
00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.798
Keep a faint copy of the old line.

56
00:02:32.798 --> 00:02:35.882
Refit with the festival week included

57
00:02:35.961 --> 00:02:38.333
the slope jumps from one point

58
00:02:38.412 --> 00:02:40.310
four to three point two.

59
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.903
And the line now misses the

60
00:02:42.986 --> 00:02:46.392
ordinary weeks too: too low at week zero

61
00:02:46.392 --> 00:02:48.220
too high at week two

62
00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.460
by more than a hundred cups each time.

63
00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.060
Least squares has no idea the

64
00:02:54.138 --> 00:02:55.768
festival was special.

65
00:02:55.768 --> 00:02:58.251
An outlier should be explained

66
00:02:58.251 --> 00:02:59.570
not averaged in.

67
00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.123
Least squares is not only for curves.

68
00:03:03.203 --> 00:03:06.497
The surveyor from Lecture eleven measured

69
00:03:06.577 --> 00:03:09.550
six climbs between five landing pads.

70
00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.796
They fit no heights

71
00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:14.262
around the right loop the

72
00:03:14.356 --> 00:03:17.580
climbs add to minus one, not zero.

73
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.090
Ground pad one at zero.

74
00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.563
Six equations, four unknown heights

75
00:03:23.563 --> 00:03:27.601
and the normal equations give four, three

76
00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:31.450
thirty one thirds and twenty six thirds.

77
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.952
The left triangle's climbs are kept exactly.

78
00:03:34.952 --> 00:03:38.032
On the right loop, each reading moves by a

79
00:03:38.104 --> 00:03:39.680
third of the mismatch.

80
00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:43.289
The residual is a multiple of the loop

81
00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:45.880
it lies in the left nullspace

82
00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.470
so the blame is spread evenly.

83
00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.357
Finally, machine learning.

84
00:03:51.357 --> 00:03:55.350
A linear regression predicts y as X times w

85
00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:58.810
with the features in the columns of X.

86
00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.855
Training on the mean squared error minimises

87
00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:05.877
the length of y minus X w, squared.

88
00:04:05.877 --> 00:04:09.320
Dividing by m does not move the minimum.

89
00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.824
So training is least squares

90
00:04:11.824 --> 00:04:15.093
and the weights solve the normal equations.

91
00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:18.139
NumPy's least squares solver returns the

92
00:04:18.213 --> 00:04:21.110
same one point nine and one point four.

93
00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.538
Neural networks use gradient

94
00:04:23.612 --> 00:04:24.799
descent instead

95
00:04:24.799 --> 00:04:27.397
small steps downhill on the error.

96
00:04:27.397 --> 00:04:28.732
On the café data

97
00:04:28.807 --> 00:04:30.810
the line walks step by step

98
00:04:30.884 --> 00:04:32.220
to the same place.

99
00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.270
Two habits carry over.

100
00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:35.738
With an intercept

101
00:04:35.738 --> 00:04:37.825
the residuals add to zero.

102
00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:40.452
And look at the largest residuals

103
00:04:40.529 --> 00:04:42.770
before you trust the weights.

104
00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:46.596
So: for any model that is linear

105
00:04:46.698 --> 00:04:50.958
in its unknowns, solve A transpose A x hat

106
00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.190
equals A transpose b.

107
00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:57.364
The error is perpendicular to every column.

108
00:04:57.364 --> 00:04:59.855
And before you trust a fit

109
00:04:59.855 --> 00:05:01.900
look at its residuals.

110
00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:03.507
Next lecture

111
00:05:03.586 --> 00:05:06.193
if the columns were perpendicular

112
00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:07.378
to begin with

113
00:05:07.378 --> 00:05:10.222
the normal equations would be easy.

114
00:05:10.222 --> 00:05:11.960
We will make them so.
